ablations/vendor/deepseek-harness/apps/cli/reference/README.md:19:`dsh --profile <name> --from-default-profile <template>` replaces the custom target with a fresh copy of one of those five shipped templates before boot. The target name cannot be a shipped profile name in any letter case. The launcher assembles the new profile in a staging directory beside the target and moves it into place, then deletes any previous target directory, including its installed dependencies and user patch. A failed move restores the previous directory. If restoring also fails, or the process stops between the two renames, the previous directory remains at `$DSH_HOME/profiles/.<name>.staging-*/previous` for manual recovery. When several processes replace the same target concurrently, at least one succeeds and the target holds one complete copy. The copy holds the template's current bundle list in a new manifest with empty dependencies and an empty user patch. It does not read the local profile named by `<template>`, copy its dependencies or patch, or persist an inheritance field; later template-list changes do not rewrite the new profile. The in-box bundles named by that copied list still resolve from the current dsh installation. A successful replacement adds no launcher output.
ablations/vendor/deepseek-harness/apps/cli/reference/README.md:60:`--dump-config-schema` uses the same bundle, profile, home, and argv patch layers as `--dump-config`, including repeatable `--patch` and `--from-default-profile`. The three dump flags are mutually exclusive and reject app arguments and the reserved `desktop` profile. Successful composition produces one pretty-printed JSON Schema 2020-12 document on stdout. Its root describes the parsed entry list printed by `--dump-config`; `$defs.patchList` separately describes profile/home/CLI overlays. Keep the document's `$defs` when validating that fragment. Preparation, patch parsing/composition, or resolution-setup failures exit nonzero without a schema. Collection and projection failures retain partial output and exit 1; diagnostics also appear on stderr. This includes `partial` projections and omitted non-JSON annotations even when stdout contains a valid, useful schema. Schema-dump unmatched-target warnings do not carry layer labels; use `--dump-config` with the same layers for source-labelled warnings.
ablations/vendor/deepseek-harness/apps/cli/reference/README.md:70:Collection imports trusted modules and may invoke Config getters and lazy builders, but never applies plugins, executes transform callbacks, or evaluates configuration expressions. Imports can block or retain process handles before or after output; automated callers should enforce an external timeout. The dump does not force-exit or own disposal of import-time resources. Ordinary import/builder stdout is routed to stderr; direct file-descriptor writes are not intercepted. Profile preparation retains YAML dump initialization writes. Only schema declarations are emitted, not actual configured values; declared defaults and raw plugin errors can still contain sensitive data. Review output before sharing. See [app-boot](../../../packages/boot/app-boot/README.md) for the collector API.
ablations/vendor/deepseek-harness/apps/cli/reference/README.md:72:Treat the output as a regenerated, pre-stable reference, not a versioned persisted catalog. `$schema` identifies the JSON Schema validation dialect; `x-cordis` follows dsh releases. Regenerate after changing dsh or plugins, and follow `$ref` and `configRef` rather than hard-coding definition names, ordering, or text. Incompatible dialect changes select a new `$schema`; annotation changes do not use Session-format migrations.
ablations/vendor/deepseek-harness/apps/cli/reference/README.md:81:New directories and files request modes `0700` and `0600` on POSIX. The CLI prints a file path only after writing succeeds. If the logs directory or file cannot be written, stderr includes the write error and full report, and the exit code remains 1. Optional-only activation warnings retain their normal output without creating a report.
