            "source_call_id": "4",
            "content": "exit_code=0\nwarning: The `fitz` API is deprecated and will be removed in future. Use `import pymupdf` instead.\nPAGES 5\n===== PAGE 1 =====\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 Structural Design Criteria Rev 2\npage 1\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 \u2014 Structural Design Criteria: Roof Diaphragm and\nPerimeter Shear Walls\nNorthern Divide Data Campus, Building A2 \u00b7 Lewis and Clark County, Montana \u00b7 Kestrel Ridge Structural Engineers, PLLC \u00b7 Revision 2, 14\nAugust 2026 \u00b7 supersedes Revision 0 of 3 May 2024\n1. Scope\nThis criteria governs the design of the Building A2 roof deck diaphragm, its chords and collectors, the precast perimeter\nshear wall panels and their connections, for wind loading. It is issued to the delegated engineer with the boundary-layer\nwind study WT-2026-07, the as-built federated model, the catalogues named in section 12, and the submittal\nspecification 01 33 00.\nSeismic design of Building A2 is governed separately by EOR-NDDC-A2-S02 and is not part of this package. The site is\nSeismic Design Category B and wind governs the lateral design of this building in both directions.\n2. Governing code\n2021 International Building Code as adopted by the State of Montana (ARM 24.301.131), with ASCE/SEI 7-16 as the\nreferenced load standard. Risk Category III per the Authority Having Jurisdiction's letter NDDC-AHJ-014. Deflection\ncriteria are taken as the more restrictive of the governing code and UFC 3-301-01 Table 2-1, which the Owner's Basis\nof Design adopts for this campus.\n3. Load basis and reporting\n3.1 Wind loads are determined at strength level from the basic wind speed of section 4 and the pressure coefficients of\nthe wind study.\n3.2 Wind is the only lateral load considered in this package. Gravity load effects on the diaphragm are not combined\nwith in-plane wind shear; dead load enters only the panel stability check of 5.8.\n3.3 Every quantity reported in this submittal - diaphragm unit shears, wall line loads and reactions, chord and\ncollector forces, deflections - is an ALLOWABLE STRESS DESIGN quantity, obtained from the strength-level\nwind effect by the load combination 0.6W. Member and connection capacities are compared against it on the\nsame basis. The drawings and the calculation package must state on their face that the loads shown are ASD.\n4. Wind parameters\n4.1 Exposure Category C, open terrain, in all directions. Enclosure classification: enclosed, GCpi = \u00b10.18. Internal\npressure acts on opposite internal faces simultaneously and therefore cancels in the net lateral force on the main wind\nforce resisting system; it is not to be included in the diaphragm and shear wall loads. It is included in the out-of-plane\npanel design, which is a separate submittal.\n4.2 The velocity pressure at elevation z is\n    q(z) = 0.00256 * Kz(z) * Kzt * Kd * Ke * V^2      [psf, V in mph]\n    Kz(z) = 2.01 * (z / 900)^(2/9.5),  z not taken less than 15 ft\nwith Kd = 0.85 and V from the AHJ letter. q_h is q(z) evaluated at the mean roof height; the velocity pressure for a\nparapet is evaluated at the top of that parapet.\n4.3 The topographic factor Kzt is taken from the site-specific study TS-2026-02 and applies over the whole footprint.\nRevision 0 of this criteria carried Kzt = 1.00 and a basic wind speed of 105 mph; both were superseded when\nthe topographic study and the AHJ letter were issued.\n4.4 The ground elevation factor is\n    Ke = exp(-0.0000362 * zg)      zg = site ground elevation above sea level, ft\nwith zg taken from the ALTA survey benchmark. Ke is not to be taken as 1.0 at this site.\n\n===== PAGE 2 =====\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 Structural Design Criteria Rev 2\npage 2\n4.5 Wind is considered from the eight azimuths at which the wind study reports coefficients, and, for the two principal\nbuilding axes, with the torsional case of 6.6. The twelve scenarios are identified as follows, and every schedule and\ndrawing in the submittal refers to them by these identifiers.\nscenario\nazimuth (from)\ncase\neccentricity e\nW01\n000 deg (N)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW02\n045 deg (NE)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW03\n090 deg (E)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW04\n135 deg (SE)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW05\n180 deg (S)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW06\n225 deg (SW)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW07\n270 deg (W)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW08\n315 deg (NW)\n1 - full pressure\n-\nW09\n000 deg (N)\n2 - torsional\n+0.15 B\nW10\n000 deg (N)\n2 - torsional\n-0.15 B\nW11\n090 deg (E)\n2 - torsional\n+0.15 B\nW12\n090 deg (E)\n2 - torsional\n-0.15 B\nThe wall lines are identified S (south, y = 0), E (east, x = 240), N1 (north, y = 180), W (west, x = 0), and, on the\ngenerator yard, N2 (y = 140) and E2 (x = 180).\n5. Precast wall panels\n5.1 The perimeter walls are precast concrete panels spanning vertically between the foundation and the roof deck, with\na parapet cantilevering above the deck. Panel-to-panel joints transfer no in-plane shear: each panel resists in-plane\nforce independently.\n5.2 Panel concrete f'c = 5000 psi, Ec = 4030 ksi, G = 0.40 Ec, unit weight 150 pcf.\n5.3 Panel geometry - mark, length, thickness, top elevation and openings - is taken from the as-built federated model\nand the roof line scan. Where the 2024 precast panel schedule and the as-built model disagree, the as-built model\ngoverns and the difference is to be reported in the design basis.\n5.4 Openings divide a panel into vertical legs. A leg is the full-height strip of panel between an opening edge and the\nnext opening edge or panel edge. A leg narrower than 2.0 ft is neglected: it is assigned no stiffness and carries no\nin-plane shear.\n5.5 Each leg is idealised as a cantilever pier fixed at the foundation and free at the roof deck, of height h equal to the\nroof deck elevation, with\n    k = 1 / ( h^3 / (3 Ec I) + 1.2 h / (G A) )        [kip/in]\n    I = t L^3 / 12,   A = t L,   t = panel thickness, L = leg length\nwith h, t and L in inches. The parapet above the deck contributes no in-plane stiffness.\n5.6 A panel's in-plane shear is the sum of the shears of its legs.\n5.7 The panel base connection to the foundation has the ASD shear capacity per foot of panel length given in the\nconnection vendor data; the demand is the panel's in-plane shear.\n5.8 Panel stability is checked with the ASD combination 0.6D + 0.6W: the overturning moment is the panel's ASD\nin-plane shear times the roof deck elevation, and the resisting moment is 0.6 times the panel self weight times half the\npanel length. Where the resisting moment is insufficient, a base tie-down assembly is provided; the assembly's ASD\ncapacity acts at a lever arm equal to the panel length. Panel self weight is computed on the GROSS panel area - full\npanel length by top elevation by thickness - with no deduction for openings.\n6. Wind pressures and the roof-level wall line load\n6.1 The wind study reports a pressure coefficient for each facade patch and each azimuth, referenced to q_h. The\ndesign pressure on a wall patch is p = q_h * Cp. The net pressure on a parapet patch is p = q_p * GCpn, with q_p the\n\n===== PAGE 3 =====\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 Structural Design Criteria Rev 2\npage 3\nvelocity pressure at the top of that parapet and GCpn the study's net parapet coefficient; the parapet coefficient already\nincludes both faces.\n6.2 Patch geometry is not tabulated in the wind study. A patch covers the full length of the panel it names and the\nelevation band its identifier names; the bands are defined in the wind study. Panel lengths and top elevations come\nfrom the as-built model.\n6.3 Wall pressures act on the gross wall area, including door and louver openings; the door and louver framing delivers\nits reaction to the surrounding panel.\n6.4 Each panel spans vertically as a simple beam between the foundation and the roof deck. A patch pressure\np acting on an area A whose centroid is at elevation z therefore delivers\n    R_roof = p * A * z / h        to the roof deck, and\n    R_base = p * A * (1 - z/h)    to the foundation\nwith h the roof deck elevation. A patch above the deck - a parapet or a screen wall - has z > h, so it delivers\nMORE than its own force to the diaphragm and an uplift-sense reaction at the base: this is the lever action of\nthe wall cantilevering above the diaphragm and it is not to be omitted. The roof-level line load on a wall line is the\nsum of R_roof over the patches of that line, per foot of wall.\n6.5 The line load acts along the wall's inward normal.\n6.6 Torsional case. For wind on either principal axis the diaphragm is also checked with all line loads factored by 0.75\ntogether with a torsional moment\n    M_T = e * B * |F|        e = +/- 0.15\nwhere |F| is the magnitude of the factored resultant in the direction of the wind and B the horizontal building dimension\nperpendicular to the wind (240 ft for wind on the north or south face, 180 ft for wind on the east or west face). M_T is\napplied to the diaphragm as a set of in-plane nodal forces proportional to the nodal tributary area and to the distance\nfrom the diaphragm area centroid, directed perpendicular to that radius, scaled so that their moment about the centroid\nequals M_T.\n7. Roof deck\n7.1 The roof is divided into the deck zones shown on S-201. Exactly one deck type from the deck manufacturer's\ncatalogue is used over the whole of each zone.\n7.2 The diaphragm unit shear anywhere within a zone must not exceed the ASD shear capacity of that zone's deck\ntype. The catalogue tabulates NOMINAL strengths; the safety factor for the ASD capacity is given in the catalogue's\nnotes.\n7.3 The catalogue's shear stiffness G' is tabulated for the deck span stated in its notes; where the framing gives a\ndifferent deck span, the note's adjustment applies. The adjustment applies to stiffness only, never to strength.\n8. Acceptance limits\n8.1 All checks are on the ASD basis of 3.3.\n8.2 Diaphragm deflection. For each scenario and each global axis, the deflection of the diaphragm relative to its\nsupports is the largest nodal displacement in that axis measured from the stiffness-weighted mean displacement of the\npiers that resist that axis. It must not exceed 0.55 in, the value the Owner's Basis of Design sets for the precast\nconnection detail.\n8.3 Pier drift must not exceed h / 400.\n9. DIAPH-MT 1.0 - the required diaphragm analysis\nThe roof deck diaphragm is neither rigid nor flexible relative to the precast walls: the walls are stiff, the wall lines are not\ncollinear, and the re-entrant corner at the generator yard has no wall on two of its sides. A tributary (flexible) or a rigid\ndistribution is not accepted for this building. The following semi-rigid model is required, and the submitted\nquantities are checked against it.\n\n===== PAGE 4 =====\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 Structural Design Criteria Rev 2\npage 4\n9.1 Mesh. A structured square mesh of side H is laid over the bounding rectangle of the roof, with its origin at the\nbuilding grid origin. A cell belongs to the diaphragm when its centre lies inside the roof outline and outside a roof\nopening. Nodes are the corners of the cells that belong. Refinement levels: L1 H = 10.0 ft, L2 H = 5.0 ft, L3 H = 2.50 ft.\n9.2 Elements. Each cell is a four-node bilinear membrane integrated with 2 x 2 Gauss quadrature, carrying the stress\nresultants\n    Nx  = EAx * eps_x        Ny = EAy * eps_y        Nxy = Ghat * gamma\n    Ghat = 12 * G'           [kip/ft, with G' in kip/in]\nEAx and EAy are the smeared axial rigidity of the roof framing, in kips per foot of width, computed as the chord area of\nthe member divided by its spacing times Es = 29,000 ksi - the girders east-west and the joists north-south, both from\nthe framing plan and the joist schedule. The deck itself is not credited with axial rigidity.\n9.3 Chords and collectors are two-node bar elements of area A and Es = 29,000 ksi on the lines of section 10, one\nbar per mesh interval, acting in the direction of the line.\n9.4 Supports. Each pier of 5.5 is a spring of stiffness k acting in the plane of its wall, attached at the mesh node\nnearest the mid-point of the leg. Where two piers resolve to the same node their stiffnesses add.\n9.5 Loads. The roof-level line load of 6.4 is applied to the perimeter nodes of its wall line as the consistent nodal loads\nof the piecewise-linear hat functions between adjacent nodes on that line.\n9.6 Recovery. The diaphragm unit shear of an element is Ghat times the engineering shear strain at the element\ncentre. The unit shear reported at a station on a wall line is that of the element which has an edge on that wall line and\nwhose centre is nearest the station. A wall line's reaction is the sum of its piers' spring forces. A chord or collector force\nis the largest bar force on that line.\n9.7 Mesh convergence. The model is run at all three levels. The convergence measure is the relative change between\nL2 and L3 in the reaction of wall line W under scenario W01, which must not exceed 5 %. The largest unit shear in the\nbuilding is a corner quantity and does not converge at that rate; report it at each level, but do not use it as the\nconvergence measure. Level L2 is the level at which the submitted quantities are reported and checked.\n9.8 Verification example VE-1\nAn implementation of DIAPH-MT must reproduce the following before it is applied to Building A2. A rectangular\ndiaphragm 100 ft (east-west) by 60 ft (north-south), mesh H = 5.0 ft, one deck type with G' = 30.0 kip/in everywhere (no\nspan adjustment), EAx = 2,900 and EAy = 13,920 kip/ft, chord bars of A = 3.00 in\u00b2 on all four edges, and twelve piers\neach of k = 500 kip/in: four on the y = 0 line and four on the y = 60 line at x = 12.5, 37.5, 62.5 and 87.5 ft resisting X,\nand two on the x = 0 line and two on the x = 100 line at y = 15 and 45 ft resisting Y. Loads: a uniform inward line load of\n0.50 kip/ft on the y = 0 line and 0.30 kip/ft on the y = 60 line, both at strength level, no torsional case.\nquantity\nvalue\nelements / nodes\n240 / 273\napplied resultant, Y\n20.00 kips\nreaction, x = 0 line\n10.00 kips\nreaction, x = 100 line\n10.00 kips\nlargest unit shear\n185.2 plf\nlargest chord bar force\n2.57 kips\nlargest displacement, Y\n0.1669 in\nVE-1 quantities are at strength level: the 0.6 factor of 3.3 has not been applied to them.\n10. Chords and collectors\n10.1 Chord and collector lines are shown on S-201 and are listed there with their identifiers. Every line carries a section\nfrom the chord and collector schedule over its full length.\n10.2 Chord force is the axial force in the chord bar; tension and compression are checked against the schedule's\nallowable values.\n\n===== PAGE 5 =====\nEOR-NDDC-A2-S01 Structural Design Criteria Rev 2\npage 5\n10.3 The collector lines at the re-entrant corner drag diaphragm shear into the walls of the generator yard; the lines\naround the mechanical well transfer shear around the opening. Both are checked as 10.2.\n11. Submittal\n11.1 A calculation package on 8-1/2 x 11 sheets, portrait, covering: the wind pressures; the parapet cantilever transfer\nto the roof deck; the diaphragm reactions; the diaphragm shears; the chord forces; the shear wall loads; and the design\nbasis.\n11.2 Plan view drawings of the roof showing the diaphragm unit shear distribution and the shear wall reactions for both\nthe transverse and the longitudinal direction, with the deck zones, the chord and collector lines, the building grid and the\nannotation required by specification 01 33 00.\n11.2.1 Report stations. The diaphragm unit shear is reported at the quarter points of every wall line - the station\nfractions 0.25, 0.50, 0.75 of the line's length, measured from the start of the line as section 4.5 sets it out - for every\nwind scenario. The unit shear at a station is recovered as 9.6 defines it.\n11.3 Schedules of the reported quantities. The Engineer of Record re-runs DIAPH-MT on the submitted design;\nreported quantities are accepted when they agree within 7.5 % or 25 plf / 2 kips / 0.05 in, whichever is larger.\n12. Reference documents\ndocument\nissued for\nWT-2026-07 Boundary Layer Wind Study\npressure coefficients, parapet coefficients\nTS-2026-02 Topographic Wind Study\nKzt\nNDDC-AHJ-014 AHJ Letter\nbasic wind speed, risk category\nALTA-2025-77 Site Survey\nsite ground elevation\nNDDC-A2 Federated Model 2026 (IFC)\nas-built geometry, panels, openings\nSR-2026-118 Roof Line Scan Report\nparapet and screen wall elevations\nA-101 Roof Plan Rev C / A-301 Elevations Rev C\narchitectural extents\nS-201 Roof Framing Plan Rev C\ndeck zones, framing, chord and collector lines\nCDS-B36 Diaphragm Design Manual\ndeck strengths, stiffness, cost\nKRS-CH Chord and Collector Schedule\nchord sections\nKRS-CN Connection Vendor Data\nembeds, base connection, tie-down\nOBD-NDDC-A2 Owner's Basis of Design\ndeflection, deck type cap, cost cap\nSpec 01 33 00 Submittal Procedures\ndeliverable format\n\n"
          }
        ]
