            "source_call_id": "5",
            "content": "exit_code=0\n########## project/40_wind/WT-2026-07_Boundary_Layer_Wind_Study.pdf PAGES 2\n===== PAGE 1 =====\nWT-2026-07 Boundary Layer Wind Study\npage 1\nWT-2026-07 \u2014 Boundary Layer Wind Study, Building A2\nSawtooth Wind Engineering \u00b7 final report \u00b7 22 July 2026 \u00b7 1:200 scale rigid model, suburban-to-open rural approach, Exposure C target profile\n1. Scope\nA pressure-model study of Building A2 in its as-built massing, including the generator-yard re-entrant corner and the\nmechanical screen wall, to provide main wind force resisting system pressure coefficients for the roof diaphragm and\nshear wall design.\n2. Model and instrumentation\nRigid pressure model at 1:200, 512 surface taps on the four elevations and the two notch elevations, sampled at 512 Hz\nfor 180 s at each azimuth, 36 azimuths at 10 degree increments. Results are reported at the eight cardinal and\nintercardinal azimuths, which envelope the measured set for every wall line.\n3. Reference pressure\nEvery coefficient in this report and in the accompanying data file is referenced to the velocity pressure at mean\nroof height, q_h. The height variation of the windward pressure is contained in the reported coefficients; the design\npressure on a wall patch is p = q_h * Cp and no further height adjustment is to be applied.\n4. Azimuth convention\nAn azimuth is the direction the wind comes FROM, measured clockwise from north: 0 degrees is a wind from the north\nblowing toward the south, 90 degrees is a wind from the east.\n5. Patch convention\nThe facade is divided into patches. A patch covers the full length of one precast panel and one elevation band. The\nbands are B1 = 0 to 7 ft, B2 = 7 to 14 ft, B3 = 14 to 21 ft, B4 = 21 to 28 ft, and the band identified P is the parapet - the\nstrip from the roof deck to the top of that panel, whatever its height. A patch identifier is the panel mark followed by\nthe band: S06-B3 is panel S06 between 14 and 21 ft, N108-P is the parapet strip of panel N108. Panel lengths and\ntop elevations are not reproduced here; they are properties of the as-built model.\n6. Coefficients\n6.1 Wall patches. The data file gives the mean pressure coefficient Cp for each wall patch and azimuth. Positive is\ntoward the surface.\n6.2 The re-entrant corner of the generator yard produces a locally amplified suction on the two notch elevations at every\nazimuth; that amplification is in the reported coefficients.\n6.3 Parapet patches. For a parapet the data file gives the NET coefficient GCpn, which acts on the parapet's projected\narea and already accounts for both faces: +1.5 on a windward parapet and -1.0 on a leeward parapet, interpolated on\nthe cosine of the incidence for an oblique wind. The net parapet pressure is p = q_p * GCpn with q_p the velocity\npressure at the top of that parapet, not at mean roof height - the screen wall on the north elevation reaches well\nabove the deck and its velocity pressure is correspondingly higher.\n6.4 Internal pressure is not part of this study.\n7. Data file\nWT-2026-07_Cp_by_azimuth.csv, columns patch_id, azimuth_deg, cp. One row per patch per azimuth.\n8. Uncertainty\n\n===== PAGE 2 =====\nWT-2026-07 Boundary Layer Wind Study\npage 2\nThe tap-to-tap repeatability of the reported means is +/- 0.03 in Cp. The coefficients are reported to four decimal places\nfor traceability, not because they are known to that precision.\n9. Limitations\nThe study covers the as-built massing of 2026. It does not cover the roof-mounted equipment, the screen wall's own\ncomponent-and-cladding pressures, or any future Building A3 to the east.\n\n########## project/40_wind/TS-2026-02_Topographic_Wind_Study.pdf PAGES 1\n===== PAGE 1 =====\nTS-2026-02 Topographic Wind Study\npage 1\nTS-2026-02 \u2014 Site-Specific Topographic Wind Study\nSawtooth Wind Engineering \u00b7 30 April 2026 \u00b7 Northern Divide Data Campus, Building A2\n1. Setting\nThe campus occupies a bench on the west side of the valley. The ground rises about 190 ft over roughly 1,500 ft from\nthe valley floor to the bench, and the parcel sits within the upper quarter of that rise.\n2. Feature classification\nThe feature is an escarpment for winds from the east through south-east and a two-dimensional ridge for winds from\nthe west. The escarpment case governs.\n3. Method\nSpeed-up was evaluated from the measured terrain profile along sixteen radials and checked against the model study's\napproach flow.\n4. Result\nThe topographic factor to be applied over the whole Building A2 footprint, at all elevations and for all azimuths,\nis Kzt = 1.19. No reduction with height is taken within the height of this building.\n5. Note on earlier work\nThe 2024 preliminary structural criteria took Kzt = 1.00 pending this study. That value is superseded.\n\n"
          }
        ]
